## Lesson catalog — bugs already hit here

### Design tokens and Tailwind v4

- **Import order in `frontend-web/app/globals.css` is load-bearing.** The generated
  tokens declare the `--ds-*` properties; the adapter maps them onto the names
  Tailwind's `@theme` reads. Reverse the two and every `var(--ds-*)` in the
  adapter points at a property that does not exist yet — which CSS resolves to
  nothing, **silently**, producing a page with no colours and no error anywhere.
- **Clearing a namespace (`--spacing-*: initial`) also removes Tailwind's bare
  multiplier**, and that is the point rather than a side effect. With the
  multiplier alive, `p-<n>` resolves for *any* n, so `p-7` renders a value
  belonging to no scale. Without it, only registered roles produce a utility and
  anything else produces nothing at all.
- **`--spacing-<key>` is consulted BEFORE `--container-<key>` for the whole
  width family** (`w-`, `max-w-`, `min-w-`, `basis-`) — undocumented, widely
  reported as
  [tailwindlabs/tailwindcss#16047](https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/issues/16047).
  A design system with `sm`/`md`/`lg` spacing keys silently hijacks `max-w-md`
  into a 40px value, which shows up as an element collapsing to zero width, not
  as anything that looks like a scale problem. This repo is immune by
  construction: spacing roles are named by job (`stack-tight`, `section-gap`),
  and a job name cannot collide with a container name. **Keep it that way** —
  the day someone adds `--spacing-md`, the bug returns.
- **`z-10` and `z-50` survive anything the theme does.** Tailwind builds bare
  numeric `z-*` arithmetically without consulting the theme, so clearing the
  namespace does not remove them and named layers only get added alongside. CSS
  cannot close this; the ESLint rule `no-unnamed-z-index-class` is the only
  place left where it can be closed.
- **No class-facing name may resolve to a primitive** — the invariant the whole
  token architecture rests on. The adapter is the only place a token is given a
  class name, so it is the only place the boundary can be broken; register
  `--color-x: var(--ds-color-neutral-700)` and the project has `bg-x` forever.
  The generator checks this on every run against the exact list of variables the
  primitive tiers emitted, **because role and primitive cannot be told apart by
  name shape** — both compile to `--ds-color-*`.
- **A named constant holding `"26px"` is still a hardcoded dimension.**
  `no-raw-dimension-value` used to inspect only a string literal inside
  `style={{}}`, so `const BOX = "1.25rem"`, `N + "px"`, `` `${N}px` ``,
  `mergeStyle`'s extra object, and an extracted `wrapperStyle` all slipped
  through — and several component READMEs documented the named identifier as
  the official exemption. An agent used that hole. The rule is now
  fail-closed: it resolves same-file identifiers, treats imports as unknown
  (flag), and the message asks for a token resource (`var(--…)` or a
  Tailwind class), not another constant. Lucide `size={16}` is a third
  channel (SVG attribute, not CSS) closed by `no-raw-icon-size`. The only
  remaining silence is a complete `@architecture-exception` that names the
  rule and an ADR (today: virtualizer `estimateSize`, ADR-042). The color
  twin (`const C = "#fff"`) is the same hole and is not closed yet.
- **A contrast decision must be made against the worst surface, not the
  obvious one.** Muted text is tightest on the *inset* surface, which is the
  lightest of the dark set, not on the darkest one. Measuring against the
  darkest gives an answer that is comfortably wrong.
- **Contrast thresholds move with font size, so the type scale and the palette
  are one problem, not two.** APCA drops its requirement at 15px and again at
  18px; the original 11–32 scale sat on the strict side of both jumps, which
  made three distinguishable levels of grey text arithmetically impossible. The
  fix was the type scale (now 15–36), not the colours.

### React, Server Components and hydration

- **A `"use client"` module is an opaque client reference, and only its
  COMPONENT exports cross the boundary correctly.** A plain constant exported
  from one arrives as `undefined` on the server — so server code reading a
  storage key from a provider module calls `localStorage.getItem(undefined)`,
  reads a key literally named `"undefined"`, and breaks with no error anywhere.
  Constants shared across the boundary go in a **directive-free file** of their
  own (`theme/provider/constants.ts`); that file's lack of `"use client"` is
  load-bearing, not untidiness.
- **State that lives outside React is read with `useSyncExternalStore`, not
  copied in with an effect.** The first version of the theme provider used an
  effect after mount. That does remove the hydration mismatch, but it costs a
  full re-render of the tree and leaves the wrong theme on screen until it runs.
  `useSyncExternalStore` exists for exactly this shape: server snapshot during
  hydration, live value immediately after, with React aware the two differ on
  purpose.
- **Nothing a test can wait for corresponds to a React effect.** `networkidle`
  tracks the network and nothing else. Any correctness that depends on an effect
  having run is therefore untestable *and* user-visible as a flash — which is
  why the theme is applied by a synchronous inline `<script>` in `<head>` that
  runs before hydration and before the first paint. The root element needs
  `suppressHydrationWarning` alongside it, because the script mutates the DOM
  before React compares it to the server's output.
- **Strict Mode remounts in development and resets the root element's class
  list to what React manages**, wiping what that inline script set. A
  `useLayoutEffect` restores it before the browser paints; a passive effect lets
  the wrong theme flash back in. This is the one place an effect is right —
  writing a class onto an element React does not own is a side effect, not
  derived state.
- A cookie read on the server was considered for theme and rejected: it opts the
  route out of static rendering, and cookie-varying HTML in a shared cache is
  how one reader's preference gets served to everyone.

### Vitest and Testing Library

- **`fireEvent.click`'s default `MouseEvent.detail: 0` is indistinguishable
  from a real Enter/Space key activation to code that branches on it** (Base
  UI's menu internals do — a real mouse click highlights nothing until an
  arrow key is pressed, a keyboard-equivalent activation pre-highlights the
  first item). A jsdom test asserting "click behavior" can therefore silently
  exercise the keyboard path instead — caught only because a Playwright run
  against real Chromium disagreed with the passing jsdom test. Pass
  `{ detail: 1 }` explicitly when the distinction matters.

### Playwright and the accessibility suites

- **`packages/storybook`'s `test:*` scripts must not unconditionally
  `build-storybook`.** A spec-only edit does not feed the iframe, but every
  `test:scoped` / `test:e2e` used to rebuild Storybook anyway — minutes of
  Vite for a Playwright file that `http-server` already had. `scripts/
  ensure-storybook-static.mjs` rebuilds only when an input (`.storybook/`,
  this package's config, `frontend-shared/src`, the workspace
  `pnpm-lock.yaml`, the repo-root `package.json`) is newer than
  `storybook-static/index.json`, when a story was deleted or renamed (the
  parent directory's mtime), or when `CI` is set. Specs under `tests/`
  are not inputs, and neither are `*.md` / `*.test.*` inside
  `frontend-shared`. There is no per-story static emit; skip vs full rebuild
  is the granularity `storybook build` actually has.
- **`page.keyboard.press` after a popup `click()` races Base UI's focus
  move.** `FloatingFocusManager` focuses the popup on the next animation
  frame (`enqueueFocus`); Playwright's `click()` returns before that.
  `expect()` retries the assertion, not the keypress, so a missed letter
  looks like `toHaveAttribute(..., "")` receiving `null` (Playwright prints
  that as "serializes to the same string"). Typeahead is the fragile case —
  ArrowDown/Home/End are also bound on the trigger. Wait for the popup to
  be focused, or `locator.press` it. Caught on
  `packages/storybook/tests/e2e/menu-keyboard.spec.ts`.
- **`webServer.url` must point at a route that actually exists.** Probing `/`
  when the app has no root page means the readiness check 404s until it times
  out, and the failure reads like a build failure, sending you to debug the
  wrong thing entirely.
- **Seed state with `addInitScript` before `goto`, never by waiting.**
  `addInitScript` runs ahead of every script the page owns, including the
  pre-hydration one. A suite that instead waits for the provider's effects lands
  inside that window at random and produces a flake that looks exactly like a
  visual regression.
- **One APCA threshold for all text is wrong** and produces a wall of false
  failures that discredits the whole check. APCA levels are per use case — body
  text, prominent text, spot-readable — and a disabled control is *supposed* to
  sit at the bottom one.
- **axe `incomplete` is not a pass.** A pair axe could not measure is an unknown
  pair, not a passing one, and it stays silent unless the spec fails on it
  explicitly.
- **Two specs attaching results under the same attachment name collide** in the
  reporter and become indistinguishable. WCAG and APCA are deliberately separate
  suites — so either can be removed without touching the other — and they need
  separate attachment names to stay that way.
- **`exactOptionalPropertyTypes` forbids assigning `undefined`** to an optional
  property. Use conditional spreading (`...(cond ? { key: value } : {})`) rather
  than assigning `undefined` and hoping.
- **`playwright test --update-snapshots <pattern>` silently eats `<pattern>`
  as the flag's own optional `[mode]` argument** — commander.js (`--update-snapshots`
  accepts `all|changed|missing|none`) greedily binds the very next token,
  so `<pattern>` never reaches the test filter and instead fails with
  `argument '<pattern>' is invalid` ([microsoft/playwright#36297](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/issues/36297)).
  Put the positional pattern before the flag: `playwright test <pattern>
  --update-snapshots`. Omitting the mode value keeps the default `changed`
  behaviour (rewrite mismatches, create missing) — the fix is only ordering,
  not a semantic change.
- **`role="presentation"` does not mean "inert" — it means "no independent
  role," which a fully real, visible, on-screen label can carry too.** Found
  hitting a genuine false exclusion: `Meter.Label`/`Progress.Label` render
  `role="presentation"` on themselves because the value is exposed through
  the meter/progressbar's own `aria-labelledby` instead, not because the text
  is decorative. A first version of the APCA text walker excluded every
  `role="presentation"` element on the (wrong) assumption that it always
  marks inert content, which silently dropped that real label from every
  sample — the correct signal for "invisible, not just role-less" is the
  rendered box's own size, not its role. Base UI's actually-inert
  `role="presentation"` span (an internal, undocumented workaround
  found on the same two components — a single character clipped to a near-1px
  box, almost certainly to satisfy an assistive-tech announcement quirk that
  needs non-empty text) is caught correctly by checking `rect.width <= 1 ||
  rect.height <= 1` instead — which also, for free, covers Tailwind's
  `sr-only` (a deliberate 1×1px box, not 0×0, so pre-2020 Safari didn't
  ignore it), so one geometry check replaces what had been two separate,
  narrower ones (a `role`-based check and a class-name-based check).
- **WCAG 1.4.3 explicitly exempts "text that is part of an inactive user
  interface component" from any contrast requirement, and axe-core already
  implements exactly that exemption** — but only for the standard signals
  (`disabled`, `aria-disabled="true"`, a disabled `<fieldset>` ancestor), not
  a project's own custom `data-disabled` styling hook. A component that only
  sets `data-disabled` for its `data-[disabled]:opacity-60` CSS gets flagged
  by axe's `color-contrast` rule for text that the standard itself does not
  require to pass — the fix is a real `aria-disabled="true"` alongside the
  existing `data-*` attribute, which is not a test workaround but a genuine,
  previously-missing accessibility signal in its own right. Confirmed by
  computing the actual WCAG relative-luminance ratio at the real 60% opacity:
  even the darkest text role in the palette lands under 3:1 once diluted that
  much, so no colour choice alone could have satisfied the letter of the
  rule — only the standard's own inactive-component exemption does.
- **axe's `color-contrast` rule reports `incomplete`, not a pass, for an
  `aria-hidden` element whose only text content is a character it cannot
  classify as a letter** (e.g. a bare `+` or `→` used as a stand-in icon
  glyph) — "Element content contains only non-text characters." A real SVG
  icon has no text node for the rule to trip on at all; reach for the
  project's own `PlaceholderIcon` convention rather than a Unicode/ASCII
  glyph in a span whenever a story needs a stand-in icon.
- **A headless library's compound part can require its full context even in
  a "just show the static styling" story.** Base UI's `Combobox.Chip`
  reads from `Combobox.Root`'s/`Chips`' internal state and throws
  (`Base UI production error #22`) if rendered as a bare child of a plain
  `<div>`, even though nothing about rendering five colour variants side by
  side actually needs a real combobox. Wrap it in the real `Root`/`Chips`
  pair with inert `items`/`value`/`onValueChange` rather than a plain
  wrapper — the context requirement does not go away just because the demo
  does not exercise selection.

### Storybook and visual regression

- **A `display=optional` Google Fonts CDN link makes a visual-regression
  baseline depend on network timing to a third party, not on anything the
  test controls.** `preview-head.html` loaded IBM Plex from
  `fonts.googleapis.com` with `&display=optional`, which gives Chromium a
  ~100ms grace period before permanently committing to the fallback font for
  that page load — so whether a run rendered the real font or the fallback
  came down to whether that one CDN fetch happened to win the race. Traced to
  a real, currently-failing CI test
  (`status-and-feedback-liveregion--polite`/`--assertive`, `mobile` project):
  the committed baseline was almost certainly captured on a losing race, while
  Windows locally, a generic Linux Docker container, and real `ubuntu-latest`
  CI all rendered the real font consistently and disagreed with it. The same
  signature (mobile-only, single theme, deterministic reflow) turned up a third
  time on `inputs-textarea--grows-with-content @ dark` while verifying the fix —
  one more stale baseline from the same cause, not a new one. Fixed by
  self-hosting both faces via `@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans`/
  `@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono` (the same self-hosted preference `next/font/
  google` already gives the two real apps) and deleting `preview-head.html`
  outright. This does not retroactively fix `feature/implement-rest-of-
  components` (PR #6): that branch's own baseline for the same two stories
  was captured against the old, non-deterministic render and needs its own
  fresh `/update-snapshots` once it rebases onto this fix — accepting a new
  baseline is a deliberate step for whoever owns that PR, not a side effect
  of this one.

### Base UI's Select/Combobox/Autocomplete family (verified while building them)

- **A mouse click needs its `pointerdown` too.** Base UI's `Select.Item`
  refuses a mouse-originated selection (`onClick`) unless a preceding
  `onPointerDown` already flipped an internal `allowMouseSelectionRef` to
  `true` — exactly what a real browser click sequence produces but a bare
  `fireEvent.click` does not. Fire `fireEvent.pointerDown(option)` immediately
  before `fireEvent.click(option)` in a test. The same family's `Enter`-to-select
  is bound to each option's own real, roving `tabindex`, not a list-level
  handler — dispatch it on the highlighted option itself, not the list
  container (unlike `ArrowDown`/`Home`/`End`/typeahead, which a list-level
  handler does own).
- **`Combobox`'s/`Autocomplete`'s trigger opens on `mousedown`, and even
  then, one animation frame late.** `useClick(..., { event: 'mousedown' })`
  wraps the actual `store.setOpen(...)` call in `requestAnimationFrame`
  (`floating-ui-react`'s own `useClick.mjs`). A test needs
  `await waitFor(() => expect(screen.getByRole("listbox"))...)` after
  `fireEvent.mouseDown(trigger)`; opening via typing or an arrow key applies
  synchronously instead, through an unrelated code path.
- **`fireEvent.change` is not a faithful simulation of real typing for this
  family.** `ComboboxInput`'s own `onChange` only opens/filters the list on a
  change whose native `InputEvent.inputType` is present and not
  `'insertReplacementText'` — real typing always sets `inputType:
  'insertText'`, but `fireEvent.change` dispatches a plain `Event` with no
  `inputType` at all, which reads as "autofill-like" and is deliberately
  ignored. Use `fireEvent.input(input, { target: { value }, inputType:
  "insertText" })`.
- **`Autocomplete`'s `ArrowDown` does not pre-highlight the first suggestion —
  by design.** Floating UI's `useListNavigation` inside `AriaCombobox.mjs` sets
  `focusItemOnOpen: false` whenever `selectionMode === 'none'` (always true for
  `Autocomplete`, since free text has no selection) and `autoHighlight` is
  unset, specifically so opening the list never visually implies a value the
  user never chose. The *first* `ArrowDown` only opens the list; the *second*
  moves the highlight to the first item. `Combobox` (`selectionMode: 'single'`)
  does not have this gap — verify per component rather than assuming parity.
- **A floating list marks the rest of the page `aria-hidden` while open, and a
  plain native `<label>` is not exempt.** Floating UI's `markOthers` utility
  (the standard WAI-ARIA combobox pattern's "hide irrelevant background
  content while expanded") applies `aria-hidden="true"` to everything outside
  the tracked reference/floating elements. A `Select.Label`/`Combobox.Label`
  is tracked and stays exempt; a plain sibling `<label htmlFor>` — the
  alternative Base UI's own forms guide offers for "input outside popup"
  compositions — is not, so the input's *computed accessible name* goes blank
  for as long as the popup is open, even though the input itself is unaffected.
  `Field.Label` avoids that gap but introduces its own: its `aria-labelledby`
  broadcasts to every labelable control inside the same `Field.Root`,
  overwriting an icon-only sub-button's own distinct `aria-label` (per the
  accessible-name-computation spec, `aria-labelledby` wins whenever both are
  present). Hold a direct element reference captured before the popup opens
  in a test, rather than re-querying by accessible name afterward.
- **A stepper button's own default `aria-label` can beat your `IconButton`'s.**
  `NumberField.Increment`/`Decrement` set `'aria-label': 'Increase'`/`'Decrease'`
  into the props Base UI merges onto whatever `render` produces; that merge
  happens *before* `IconButton`'s own `aria-label={label}` is computed inside
  its own body, so by the time the two collide, Base UI's default already
  arrived as an explicit incoming prop and wins. Set `aria-label` explicitly on
  the outer Base UI part itself (`<BaseNumberField.Increment aria-label={label}
  render={...}>`), which participates in the merge a step earlier, where an
  explicit prop still beats Base UI's own internal default. Verified by
  rendering and asserting the accessible name directly — `Select`'s/`Combobox`'s
  own equivalent buttons carry no such internal default and need no such
  extra step, confirmed the same way rather than assumed to differ.

### Lint, architecture rules and the toolchain

- **pnpm resolves a workspace package as a symlink to its source, not into
  `node_modules`.** A dependency-cruiser rule matching `node_modules/<pkg>`
  therefore never fires — the rule looks correct, reports nothing, and gives
  false confidence. Match the relative source path instead, and always verify a
  new rule by deliberately breaking it once.
- **ESLint 10:** `eslint-plugin-import` does not support it; use
  `eslint-plugin-import-x`, the maintained fork.
- **`eslint-plugin-boundaries` v7** renamed most of its config: `element-types`
  → `dependencies`, `rules` → `policies`, `mode: "full"` → `partialMatch: false`,
  and string selectors → objects. The old shape still runs, emitting a wall of
  deprecation warnings rather than failing.
- **Type-aware rules must be scoped to files a tsconfig actually covers.**
  Applying `recommendedTypeChecked` project-wide makes the flat config's own
  `.mjs` files explode, since nothing type-checks them.
- **Steiger:** an empty FSD segment fails `fsd/public-api` — give it an
  `index.ts`, not a `.gitkeep`. `providers` is a banned segment name.
- **Generated output must be excluded from every checker**, not just from git:
  the compiled token tree, `.next/`, `playwright-report/`, `test-results/`.
- **The FSD layer cannot be named `pages/`** — it collides with Next.js's legacy
  Pages Router auto-detection. This repo uses `views/`, applied at scaffolding
  time; do not "fix" it back.
- **`.cursor/hooks/enforce-frontend-commands.mjs` fails closed on at least one
  otherwise-harmless command** (`git diff --stat` reproducibly returns "hook
  returned no output" and gets blocked; `git status --short`/`git diff`
  without `--stat` do not). Not yet root-caused — recorded here so the next
  person hitting it does not re-diagnose from scratch. Work around it by using
  a different git invocation rather than retrying the same one.
- **A rule's own "always truthy" claim is only as good as the type it is
  checked against, and a hand-widened `unknown`/`as` cast at a call site can
  make the type lie.** `no-unnecessary-condition` flagged `value &&` as
  redundant in `resolveTree`'s `typeof value === "object"` branch because
  `TokenTree`'s declared leaf type has no `boolean`/`null` member — but a real
  caller elsewhere in the package passes one through via `as unknown as
  TokenTree` (`references.test.ts`'s own "passes a boolean/null leaf through
  untouched" case), and `typeof null === "object"` in JS. Removing the guard
  passed typecheck and lint, and only failed at `pnpm test` with `Cannot
  convert undefined or null to object` — the guard came back, with an inline
  `eslint-disable` citing the test by name, because the type is the thing
  that's incomplete here, not the runtime check. **Run the actual test suite
  after a "just removing a redundant check" lint fix — typecheck passing is
  not evidence the check was truly redundant.**
- **`no-invalid-void-type`'s `allowInGenericTypeArguments` does not apply to a
  call expression's explicit type argument** (`fn<void>()`), only to a type
  reference (`Promise<void>`) — a known, still-open upstream limitation
  ([typescript-eslint#6340](https://github.com/typescript-eslint/typescript-eslint/issues/6340),
  [#11997](https://github.com/typescript-eslint/typescript-eslint/issues/11997)).
  A generic test helper called as `deferred<void>()` trips it regardless of
  the option. Fixed by giving the helper's type parameter a `= void` default
  and calling it as plain `deferred()` — the default never appears as a
  literal `void` keyword at the call site, so the rule has nothing to flag,
  and the fix reads as an improvement on its own merits besides.
- **`prefer-nullish-coalescing` suggests `??` for any `x ? x : y` shape without
  checking whether `x`'s type includes a non-nullish falsy value.** For
  `x: string | undefined`, `x ? x : y` and `x ?? y` disagree exactly when `x`
  is `""` — the ternary falls through to `y`, `??` does not. Taking the
  rule's own suggestion here would have silently reintroduced a bug already
  fixed on purpose (an empty-after-trim message printing as a blank reason
  instead of falling back to the caller's default) — worth an inline
  `eslint-disable` with the reason spelled out, not a mechanical accept.
- **A headless library's `style`/`className` prop can be a plain value OR a
  function of component state** (`@base-ui/react`'s `BaseUIComponentProps`:
  `style?: CSSProperties | ((state: State) => CSSProperties | undefined)`,
  same for `className`) — `no-misused-spread` flagging `{ ...style, width,
  height }` here is a real bug catch, not noise: spreading a function copies
  none of its own enumerable properties, so a caller's per-state style
  callback silently vanishes and only the component's own additions survive.
  Fixed once, generically, as `mergeStyle` in `shared/lib` (resolves the
  caller's value first, static or not, then layers the fixed extras on top,
  returning a function unconditionally since Base UI accepts that
  natively) rather than five separate `typeof style === "function"` checks.
- **`max-lines-per-function`/`max-lines`/`max-classes-per-file` measure the
  shape of a *test file*, not a responsibility, when applied to `describe`/
  `it` structure or an inline jsdom mock class — both are legitimate shapes a
  test takes, not the smell these three rules exist to catch in real code.**
  Exempt exactly these three rules for `*.test.{ts,tsx}`/`*.spec.{ts,tsx}`/
  `*.stories.tsx`, not the rest of the proxy set (a genuinely complex test
  *function*, or a real duplicated inline class string, is still worth
  flagging).
- **Splitting one long function into several smaller ones can just move the
  line count to the file level instead of removing it.** Decomposing
  `DesignSystemStorybook`'s 154-line body into 13 small section components
  fixed `max-lines-per-function` but pushed the *file* over `max-lines` (310
  of 300) — each new function's own boilerplate (signature, braces, JSDoc)
  adds lines even as it removes complexity. Re-run the linter after a
  decomposition rather than assuming a smaller function means a smaller
  problem; the fix here was a second split, data into its own file and
  section components into another, not reversing the first split.

### Local development on Windows

- The shell is **PowerShell**, not bash. `&&` works in modern PowerShell but
  quoting rules differ; paths containing spaces need double quotes.
- A dev server that accepts connections but never responds to any request,
  including static ones, after many rapid restarts is stale process or socket
  state, not a code bug. Kill all `node` processes, clear `.next/`, retry on a
  fresh port before assuming the code broke.

### Gradle included-build plugins

- **A feature module's five Gradle projects are not a template for a Gradle
  plugin, and neither is one package of nested types.** `tallyvane.graph`
  stays one Gradle project (`ARCHITECTURE.md` §4.2). Inside it the cut is
  `domain` / `application` / `infrastructure`, the same layer names as a
  feature module, because those names say what may know YAML or Gradle.
  `manifest` / `rules` / `gradle` looked like layers and were not: they
  named a technology, so SnakeYAML still sat next to the comparison rules.
  Flattening everything into one package to match an Android plugin's
  vocabulary dump put `ModulesYaml.File` (a driver) on the port. Extra
  Gradle modules (`:graph:domain`) plus `explicitApi()` only existed to
  make `public` look load-bearing. The plugin class lives at the package
  root (`tallyvane.gradle.graph.ModuleGraphPlugin`), not under `.plugin`.
- **`kotlin-dsl`'s `Action { }` is a receiver lambda (`T.() -> Unit`), not
  SAM conversion.** `Action { graph -> graph.group = … }` therefore fails
  to compile in a `.gradle.kts` / kotlin-dsl source set. Binary plugin
  sources on `java-gradle-plugin` + `kotlin-jvm` do not get that helper, so
  the same `Action { graph -> }` form is the Java SAM and is the one to
  use there. Do not add `GraphTaskConvention`-style wrappers to dodge a
  receiver that is not on that classpath.
- **A custom ktlint autocorrect must mutate the AST with `raw*` methods,
  not `PsiElement.replace` / `KtPsiFactory`.** ktlint's dummy PSI does not
  install IntelliJ's `treeCopyHandler`; those PSI APIs throw
  `IllegalArgumentException: Missing extension point` the moment they run,
  including under `ktlint-test`. `rawReplaceWithText` exists only on a
  leaf (`BLOCK_COMMENT`, `EOL_COMMENT`). KDoc is a composite
  `LazyParseablePsiElement` — replace the node with
  `TreeElement.rawReplaceWithList`. Style that official ktlint does not
  cover lives in `backend/build-logic/ktlint-rules`, not in detekt or
  Konsist (ADR-043).

### Backend architecture

- **A nested `Jobs.Fake` in `src/main` is production code.** Kotlin compiles it
  to `Jobs$Fake.class` in the published jar, so every consumer of the port
  ships a test double. The double is `JobsFake` in that module's `src/test`.
  `Cached` / `Retrying` still nest: they are production. Konsist
  `no-fake-in-main` (ADR-044) is the gate; `fake-is-nested` was the opposite
  mistake.

